Also, if "collective" means "all together" and "distributive" means
"one by one" or "individually", there are a lot of other options in
between: in pairs, in threes, some in pairs and some individually, and
so on. "All together" and "one by one" are just the two extremes. If
we only have forms for the extremes we are left with no form for all
the intermediate cases, and if we do have a form that doesn't
distinguish between all the intermediate possibilities there is no
reason to exclude one of the extremes from that neutral form.

> This role was given to simple forms, without external quantifiers. =A0The=
n the
> somewhat shakier thought came along that we didn't really need a separate=
 form
> for collective use, since the use of the simple form was generally clear =
and,
> when not, the matter was easily rectified.

There's still "loi" and "lo'u". But if "loi pano verba cu bevri loi
pano stuzi lo purdi" means that the ten children together carried the
ten chairs bundled together, we still need a way to say that the ten
children carried the ten chairs to the garden without having to
specify whether they each took one chair, or they worked in pairs, or
some worked in groups and others individually, and so on. Maybe all we
care to say or all we know is that they took the chairs to the garden,
without saying or knowing how the job was distributed.

